\begin{equation}
 X_x-iX_y=z^m Q(z),\qquad Q(0)\ne0 .
 \label{eq:annular-boundary-factor}
\end{equation}
Here $Q$ is continuous, locally half-Holder, and has weak derivative
in $L^2$; it is $C^1$ in the interior.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The complex differential satisfies $\bar\partial\Phi=A\Phi$.
A small-disk Cauchy-transform contraction gives an invertible
gauge in which it is holomorphic. At the boundary, a metric row
frame and reflection by its anti-complex involution reduce to the
same construction. Holomorphic factorization gives
\eqref{eq:annular-boundary-factor} and the stated estimates.

Local vanishing would propagate to a constant disk, contradicting
the Jordan trace. Zeros are therefore isolated relative to the
closed disk; compactness makes them finite. A noninjective monotone
label would collapse an arc, on which the tangential differential,
and by conformality the full differential, would vanish.
Thus the label is injective and hence a homeomorphism.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:annular-plateau}]
\proves{thm:annular-plateau}
The preceding lemmas give the same conformal harmonic minimum
with a homeomorphic boundary label and within-$C^1$ regularity.
Compactness makes it Lipschitz. Hence it is an actual admissible
disk, and
\[
 A_g(\gamma)\leq\operatorname{Area}_g(u)=E(u)\leq A_g(\gamma).
\]
\end{proof}
\source{MorreyICM1950}\dcref{Sections 3--5, pp. 181--187}
\source{MT2007}\dcref{Lemma 19.2, pp. 437--439}

